<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="71445" href="schedule/2019/fall/ANTH/399/71445.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2019" href="schedule/2019.xml">2019</calendarYear><term id="120198" href="schedule/2019/fall.xml">Fall 2019</term><subject id="ANTH" href="schedule/2019/fall/ANTH.xml">Anthropology</subject><course id="399" href="schedule/2019/fall/ANTH/399.xml">Special Topics</course></parents><sectionNumber>KC1</sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Birth &amp; Post Partum Period</sectionTitle><creditHours>3 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>This course offers a biocultural approach to the study of pregnancy, birth, and the postpartum period. In particular, we will focus on the elements of these physiological events that are the most underground and least discussed. We will tackle not only the biology of these experiences, but how they are represented, the kinds of variation birthing people experience, and what that means for the future of research and medicine in this area.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2019-08-26Z</startDate><endDate>2019-12-11Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>04:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>T      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>209A</roomNumber><buildingName>Davenport Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Clancy" firstName="K">Clancy, K</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>